= Half-GCD candidate-root updates
T-1800 • 2026-11-07 • PCC-20261107-73b448 • unsigned producer evidence

Candidate-root recovery now uses the existing recursive half-GCD engine for later sign constraints. A new capped driver returns a monic gcd and handles zero, constant, swapped and equal-degree inputs. The moment converter, seed construction, sign source and final scalar-to-point check are preserved.

#image("flow.svg", width: 100%)

== Why the candidate set is preserved
Each Euclidean step is a polynomial matrix with determinant −1. Products of these matrices preserve the ideal generated by the input pair and therefore its gcd up to a unit. The existing half-GCD applies its matrix back to the full inputs and checks the degree reduction; final monic normalization fixes the representative.

For a squarefree split candidate polynomial, gcd with the next shifted character constraint retains precisely the matching roots. A zero sign uses X+a. The empty polynomial1 is absorbing. Recovery accepts a singleton only after checking its point equality.

